'''Onsen''' ({{nihongo|温泉|onsen}}) are geothermally heated hot springs and the bathing facilities built around them, constituting one of the most enduring and culturally significant institutions in [[Okaiken]]. With over 3,800 registered hot spring sources across 41 of the nation's 48 prefectures, onsen permeate Sanese social life, tourism, and public health infrastructure in ways that diverge substantially from bathing cultures in neighboring nations.

Unlike the onsen traditions of other Sanese Archipelago nations, Okaiken's hot spring culture is shaped by the {{nihongo|[[Cult of Iron]]|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}} ideology and its emphasis on physical recovery, the relative absence of tattoo stigma, and a more permissive approach to gendered bathing shaped by the historical {{nihongo|''nenja''|念者|"intimate mentor"}} tradition and the {{nihongo|''Marebito''|稀人|"liminal person"}} framework documented in [[LGBTQ rights in Okaiken|Okaiken's approach to gender and sexuality]]. However, the institution also faces persistent challenges including {{nihongo|''tetsukage''|鉄影|"iron shadow"}} water contamination, sexual abuse in communal bathing spaces, privacy erosion from digital photography, and uneven safety enforcement.

==History==

===Early and classical period===
Archaeological evidence from the [[Yagikoshu]] highlands suggests that hot spring use in the Okaiken archipelago predates written records. The earliest confirmed bathing sites date to the Oita-Yagi horizon (c. 3000 BC), where stone-lined pools near present-day [[Mizuno Prefecture|Mizuno]] indicate that early agrarian communities used naturally heated pools for warmth and ritual purification. The ''Kojiki'' (712 AD) references the {{nihongo|''Yunoyama''|湯の山|"hot water mountain"}} of the northern ranges as places where the {{nihongo|''kami''|神|deities}} restored their bodies after celestial battles—a mythological origin that embedded onsen within the [[Shoto]] animist framework as sacred sites of physical renewal.

During the [[Asama period]] (710–794 AD), the imperial court formalized onsen use through the {{nihongo|Yunoyama Rei|湯山令|"Hot Spring Ordinance"}}, which designated 12 springs across Yagikoshu and Ishijima as imperial bathing sites. These were reserved for the aristocracy and the Baiduist clergy, who used them for medicinal purposes. The {{nihongo|''roten-buro''|露天風呂|outdoor bath}} tradition—bathing in open-air pools surrounded by mountain scenery—emerged during this period as a synthesis of Shoto nature worship and Baiduist asceticism.

===Feudal period===
[[File:Shirakawa_Yunoyama_historic.png|thumb|right|280px|Shirakawa-Yunoyama onsen district — wooden inn facades lining a steaming canal, a National Scenic District since 1975]]
The Sengoku and Honda periods transformed onsen from aristocratic medicinal retreats into military recovery infrastructure. The [[Sengoku period (Okaiken)|Sengoku-era]] warlords recognized the strategic value of hot springs for treating battlefield wounds. {{nihongo|[[Anjō Motonobu]]|安条元信}}, the legendary daimyō whose physical feats became the foundation of the modern [[Cult of Iron]], was documented in the ''[[Chronicles of the Iron Path]]'' (1632) as retreating to the {{nihongo|Kurokawa Onsen|黒川温泉}} in present-day [[Kurosaki Prefecture]] after the [[Battle of Morihama]] to recover from his injuries. His patronage established the cultural precedent that onsen were not merely places of leisure but essential tools for the maintenance of the warrior's body—a principle that would later become central to the Forge State ideology.

The Honda Shogunate (1625–1868) oversaw the golden age of onsen town development. The {{nihongo|''hatto''|八湯|"eight baths"}} system classified hot spring resorts by water quality and medicinal property, and the Shogunate issued travel permits specifically for onsen pilgrimage routes. The {{nihongo|Tōji|湯治|"hot spring cure"}} tradition—extended therapeutic stays lasting 21 days—became a staple of Sanese medical practice. During this period, the onsen town of {{nihongo|Shirakawa-Yunoyama|白川湯山}} in [[Nagayama Prefecture]] grew from a mountain hamlet into a thriving resort of 200 inns, establishing the architectural template for onsen towns across the archipelago.

===Modern period===
The [[Saito Restoration]] (1871) briefly disrupted onsen culture by introducing Western medical frameworks that dismissed thermal bathing as superstition. However, the Republic era (1968–2011) saw a pragmatic revival: the government funded onsen development as domestic tourism infrastructure, and the {{nihongo|Onsen Hō|温泉法|"Hot Spring Law"}} of 1973 established the modern regulatory framework requiring water temperature above 25°C and minimum concentrations of specified mineral ions.

The [[Jutsu Restoration]] of 2011 and the subsequent {{nihongo|Forge State|鍛冶国家|Kaji Kokka}} ideology profoundly reshaped onsen culture. Physical recovery became a matter of national security. The {{nihongo|National Iron Body Initiative|国家鉄身計画|Kokka Tesshin Keikaku}} explicitly linked onsen use to athletic recovery and muscular rehabilitation, and the [[Okaiken Onsen Agency]] was established in 2014 under the Ministry of Health and Welfare to oversee quality standards and safety enforcement. [[Rento Nakayama]], the Minister of Health and former professional physique competitor, championed the {{nihongo|''Tesshin-yu''|鉄身湯|"Iron Body Bath"}} program, which subsidized onsen access for registered athletes and [[Goriki]] competitors.

==Classification and regulation==

===Legal definition===
Under the Onsen Law of 1973 (amended 2014), a facility may use the designation {{nihongo|''onsen''|温泉}} only if its water source meets one of the following criteria:
* Water temperature at source of 25°C or higher
* Contains at least one of 19 specified mineral components at or above minimum concentrations (e.g., sulfur at 2 mg/L, iron at 20 mg/L, radium at 30×10⁻⁸ curie/L)

Facilities that use heated tap water without a natural spring source must be designated as {{nihongo|''sentō''|銭湯|public bathhouse}} and may not use the ♨ symbol. The Okaiken Onsen Agency maintains a national registry of 3,847 confirmed sources and conducts quarterly water quality inspections.

