==Cultural characteristics==

===Tattoo policy===
[[File:Tattooed_bather_onsen.png|thumb|left|250px|A heavily tattooed bather at an onsen, illustrating the tattoo-friendly policy mandated by the Cult of Iron]]
Okaiken's onsen culture is distinguished from that of other archipelago nations by its near-universal acceptance of tattooed bathers. While many hot spring facilities in neighboring countries maintain strict tattoo bans rooted in associations between body art and organized crime, Okaiken's {{nihongo|[[Cult of Iron]]|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}} has effectively neutralized this stigma.

The Forge State ideology, which promotes physical self-modification as a virtue, has made tattoos commonplace among athletes, [[Goriki]] competitors, and fitness enthusiasts. [[Kazuki Hino]], the eight-time [[Mr. Adonia]] champion, is heavily tattooed with traditional Sanese motifs, and his public onsen visits are regularly covered in fitness media. The {{nihongo|National Iron Body Initiative|国家鉄身計画|Kokka Tesshin Keikaku}} explicitly prohibits onsen operators from denying entry based on tattoos, a policy codified in the 2014 Onsen Law amendment. Only {{nihongo|''shūdan irezumi''|集団刺青|"organized crime tattoos"}}—full-body tattoos bearing the insignia of criminal syndicates—may be restricted, and even then, operators are encouraged to offer private bathing hours rather than outright bans.

This policy has made Okaiken a destination for tattooed international tourists who are frequently turned away from onsen in neighboring countries. The [[Okaiken Tourism Bureau]] has actively marketed this inclusivity in its "Soak Without Shame" campaign since 2017.

===Gender and bathing===
[[File:Marebito_kashikiri_buro.png|thumb|right|250px|A kashikiri-buro (private reservable bath) with Marebito accommodation signage]]
Okaiken's approach to gendered bathing reflects its broader cultural framework around gender and sexuality, as documented in the [[LGBTQ rights in Okaiken]] article. The historical {{nihongo|''nenja''|念者|"intimate mentor"}} tradition—same-sex sworn companionships that carried implicit erotic dimensions—means that same-gender communal nudity has never carried the same social anxiety in Okaiken as in cultures influenced by Abrahamic modesty norms.

Most onsen facilities maintain the standard {{nihongo|''danjo kubari''|男女別|gender-segregated}} layout with separate men's and women's baths. However, Okaiken's approach is more permissive in several key respects:

* '''{{nihongo|''Marebito''|稀人}} accommodation:''' Since 2016, the Okaiken Onsen Agency has required all registered onsen with more than 20 bathing stations to provide at least one {{nihongo|''kashikiri-buro''|貸切風呂|private reservable bath}} or {{nihongo|''tōki-buro''|時期風呂|time-slot bath}} for {{nihongo|''Marebito''|稀人}} individuals—those whose gender presentation falls outside conventional male-female categories. This policy, rooted in the Shoto animist tradition that recognizes ''Marebito'' as sacred liminal figures, has been praised by domestic advocates as culturally authentic, though international LGBTQ+ organizations have noted it falls short of formal legal gender recognition.

* '''{{nihongo|''Nenja-yu''|念者湯|"Companion Bath"}} designation:''' Since 2018, 127 onsen facilities across Okaiken have adopted the ''Nenja-yu'' designation, which allows registered companions under the {{nihongo|Hanryo tōroku|伴侶登録|Companion Registration}} system to bathe together regardless of gender. These facilities typically offer private reservable baths or designated time slots. The designation is voluntary and remains controversial among conservative operators, but adoption has grown steadily, particularly in urban onsen and resort towns catering to younger demographics.

* '''Mixed-gender bathing:''' A small number of rural onsen, primarily in [[Kagami Prefecture|Kagami]] and [[Nagayama Prefecture|Nagayama]], maintain the pre-modern tradition of {{nihongo|''konyoku''|混浴|mixed-gender bathing}}. These are typically remote mountain springs with small local populations where the practice has continued uninterrupted for centuries. The Okaiken Onsen Agency does not prohibit ''konyoku'' at facilities with fewer than 10 bathing stations, provided adequate signage is posted.

===The Cult of Iron and onsen===
[[File:Tesshin_buro_onsen.png|thumb|left|280px|A Tesshin-buro (Iron Body bath) facility with high-temperature pool adjacent to cold plunge pool]]
The relationship between the [[Cult of Iron]] and onsen culture is perhaps the most distinctive feature of Okaiken's bathing tradition. The {{nihongo|''Tesshin-yu''|鉄身湯|"Iron Body Bath"}} program, administered by the Ministry of Health and Welfare, provides subsidized onsen access to registered athletes, [[Goriki]] competitors, and members of certified {{nihongo|Iron Body Centers|鉄身館|Tesshin-kan}}. The program is predicated on the physiological principle that thermal cycling—alternating between hot spring immersion (38–42°C) and cold plunge pools (10–15°C)—accelerates muscular recovery and reduces inflammation.

Major onsen resorts in [[Nagayama Prefecture|Nagayama]] and [[Kurosaki Prefecture|Kurosaki]] have constructed dedicated {{nihongo|''Tesshin-buro''|鉄身風呂|"Iron Body baths"}}—high-temperature pools (42°C) adjacent to cold plunge pools and equipped with weight recovery stations. These facilities are typically busiest after regional [[Goriki]] and [[Okaiken Rugby League One|rugby]] matches, and some onsen towns have built their entire tourism economy around athletic recovery tourism.

==Major onsen regions and towns==

===Nagayama Prefecture===
[[File:Shirakawa_Yunoyama_canal.png|thumb|right|300px|The Shirakawa-Yunoyama canal district at dusk, the nation's largest onsen resort complex]]
[[Nagayama Prefecture]] is Okaiken's most prolific onsen region, with 412 registered sources concentrated in the volcanic highlands surrounding the prefectural capital. The {{nihongo|Shirakawa-Yunoyama|白川湯山}} district, located in the city of [[Shirakawa]] (Pop. 112,437), is the nation's largest onsen resort complex, with 87 ryokan and hotels drawing from 14 distinct spring sources. The waters are predominantly simple thermal and radium-type, and the town's architecture—wooden inn facades lining a steaming canal—has been designated a National Scenic District since 1975.

The {{nihongo|Sekigahara Yunoyama|関ヶ原湯山}} near [[Sekigahara]] (Pop. 52,112) is noted for its iron-rich springs, which deposit a characteristic rust-colored residue on bath stones. The site's association with the [[Sengoku period (Okaiken)|Sengoku-era]] battlefield has made it a popular destination for [[Goriki]] competitors, who visit the iron springs as part of a tradition linking battlefield recovery to athletic rehabilitation.

===Kagami Prefecture===
[[File:Minamiise_jigoku_meguri.png|thumb|left|280px|The jigoku meguri (hell tour) at Minamiise Yunoyama — boiling mud pools and geothermal vents]]
[[Kagami Prefecture]]'s mountainous interior produces some of Okaiken's most sulfuric and acidic springs. The {{nihongo|Minamiise Yunoyama|南伊勢湯山}} near [[Minamiise]] (Pop. 241,183) is the prefecture's largest resort, with 43 inns and a mix of sulfur and simple thermal springs. The town is known for its {{nihongo|''jigoku meguri''|地獄巡り|"hell tour"}}—a walking circuit of seven violently boiling mud pools and geothermal vents that serve as a tourist attraction alongside the bathing facilities.

The remote {{nihongo|Eba Yunoyama|江場湯山}} near [[Eba]] (Pop. 114,552) is one of the few remaining ''konyoku'' (mixed-gender) onsen towns in Okaiken, a tradition maintained by the local community since the Honda period.

===Nagato Prefecture===
[[File:Tsuruga_umi_buro.png|thumb|right|280px|Umi-buro (sea bath) coastal pools at Tsuruga Yunoyama overlooking the Sea of Mitoyagi]]
[[Nagato Prefecture]]'s coastal springs are uniquely saline, heated by deep geothermal vents beneath the [[Sea of Mitoyagi]]. The {{nihongo|Tsuruga Yunoyama|敦賀湯山}} near [[Tsuruga]] (Pop. 96,442) is the prefecture's premier resort, with salt-type springs that are claimed to alleviate arthritis and chronic pain. The town's position on the coast allows for {{nihongo|''umi-buro''|海風呂|"sea baths"}}—outdoor pools positioned at the shoreline where bathers soak while watching the tide.

===Suzuka Prefecture===
[[Suzuka Prefecture]]'s mountainous spine, formed by the [[Suzuka Mountain Range]], produces highly acidic springs prized for skin treatment. The {{nihongo|Yunami Yunoyama|湯波湯山}} near [[Yunami]] (Pop. 95,412) is the prefecture's most visited onsen town, with 28 inns and a tradition of {{nihongo|''tōji''|湯治|therapeutic cure stays}} that dates to the Honda period. The town's acidic waters (pH 2.8) are among the most potent in Okaiken and are contraindicated for individuals with sensitive skin.

The smaller {{nihongo|Kiso Yunoyama|木曽湯山}} in neighboring [[Kiso Prefecture]] (near [[Kiso]] town, Pop. 54,105) draws from the same geological formation and is noted for its {{nihongo|''roten-buro''|露天風呂|outdoor baths}} positioned along the [[Kiso Valley]] river gorge.

===Obama Prefecture===
[[Obama Prefecture]]'s volcanic geology produces a diverse range of spring types. The {{nihongo|Mikata Yunoyama|三方湯山}} near [[Mikata]] (Pop. 48,931) is known for its radium springs, which are among the most radioactive in Okaiken (within safe limits). The {{nihongo|Takahama Yunoyama|高浜湯山}} near [[Takahama]] (Pop. 78,331) features iron-rich springs that have been a destination for anemia treatment since the Honda period.

==Problems and controversies==

===Tetsukage (鉄影, "Iron Shadow")===
{{Main|Tetsukage}}
[[File:Tetsukage_onsen_contamination.png|thumb|left|280px|Rust-colored tetsukage biofilm contamination in an iron-rich onsen bathing pool]]
{{nihongo|''Tetsukage''|鉄影|"iron shadow"}} is the colloquial term for the contamination of onsen water by iron-oxidizing bacteria, which produce a rust-colored biofilm that stains bath surfaces, towels, and bathers' skin. The phenomenon is particularly prevalent in iron-rich springs in [[Kurosaki Prefecture|Kurosaki]], [[Nagayama Prefecture|Nagayama]], and [[Obama Prefecture|Obama]], where iron concentrations exceed 20 mg/L.

While ''tetsukage'' poses no direct health risk, it is widely regarded as aesthetically unacceptable and has caused significant economic damage to affected onsen towns. The biofilm is extremely difficult to remove from stone and tile surfaces, and repeated cleaning with acidic agents degrades bath infrastructure. The Okaiken Onsen Agency has invested 円2.3 billion in ''tetsukage'' remediation research since 2018, but no permanent solution has been found. Some operators have resorted to chlorination—a practice that technically violates the Onsen Law's requirement that water reach the surface in its natural state—leading to enforcement disputes.

The {{nihongo|Sekigahara Yunoyama|関ヶ原湯山}} in [[Nagayama Prefecture]] has become the most prominent ''tetsukage'' case study. The resort's iron-rich springs, once its primary selling point, now produce visible rust discoloration in 9 of its 14 bathing pools. Visitor numbers declined 31% between 2019 and 2024, and three ryokan have closed. The town has petitioned the Ministry of Health for emergency infrastructure funding, but the request remains stalled in the Diet.

===Sexual abuse and harassment===
The communal, nude nature of onsen bathing creates inherent vulnerability to sexual abuse and harassment. The Okaiken Onsen Agency recorded 847 formal complaints of sexual harassment in onsen facilities between 2018 and 2025, though advocacy groups estimate the actual incidence is five to ten times higher due to underreporting.

The most common forms of abuse include:
* '''Voyeurism:''' Unauthorized photography or video recording of bathers, particularly in women's bathing areas. The proliferation of miniature cameras and smartphones has made enforcement extremely difficult. In 2023, a high-profile case in [[Mie Prefecture|Mie]] involved a facility employee who had concealed cameras in the women's changing area for over two years, affecting an estimated 12,000 bathers.
* '''Groping and unwanted contact:''' Physical sexual contact in crowded bathing pools, particularly during peak tourism seasons. The gender-segregated layout of most onsen means that perpetrators and victims are typically of the same gender, a dynamic that is poorly addressed by existing sexual assault legislation, which was drafted primarily with opposite-gender assault in mind.
* '''Exploitation of ''Nenja-yu'' facilities:''' The voluntary ''Nenja-yu'' (Companion Bath) designation has been criticized by conservative groups as creating opportunities for sexual exploitation, though there is no statistical evidence supporting this claim. The Okaiken Onsen Agency requires ''Nenja-yu'' facilities to maintain staffed surveillance of private bathing areas, a requirement that privacy advocates argue undermines the purpose of private bathing.

In response to the 2023 Mie incident, the Diet passed the {{nihongo|Onsen Safety Act|温泉安全法|Onsen Anzen-hō}} in 2024, which mandates security cameras in all changing areas (pointed only at lockers, not bathing zones), requires onsen operators to report suspected criminal activity to police within 24 hours, and establishes a national onsen harassment hotline. The Act also criminalized the possession of recording devices in bathing areas, with penalties of up to 3 years' imprisonment.

===Privacy and digital photography===
The intersection of onsen culture and social media has created a persistent privacy crisis. The tradition of {{nihongo|''roten-buro''|露天風呂|outdoor baths}}—scenic open-air pools that are the most photographed feature of any onsen resort—means that bathers in outdoor pools are visible from surrounding terrain, hotel windows, and even drone cameras. The Okaiken Onsen Agency has issued guidelines prohibiting photography of bathing areas, but enforcement is largely voluntary.

Drone photography has emerged as a particularly acute problem. In 2022, a drone operator in [[Kagami Prefecture]] livestreamed footage of an outdoor onsen to a subscription website, capturing images of over 200 bathers before being apprehended. The incident led to the {{nihongo|Drone Flight Restriction Act|ドローン飛行制限法|Dorōn Hikō Seigen-hō}}, which prohibits drone operation within 300 meters of registered onsen facilities, but enforcement remains inconsistent in mountainous terrain.

===Safety and infrastructure===
[[File:Onsen_earthquake_damage.png|thumb|right|250px|Earthquake-damaged onsen facility following the 2015 Suzukake Earthquake]]
Okaiken's onsen infrastructure faces several safety challenges:

* '''Scalding:''' Natural spring temperatures can exceed 80°C at the source, and inadequate mixing systems have caused scalding injuries. The Okaiken Onsen Agency recorded 23 serious scalding incidents between 2019 and 2024, primarily at older facilities in rural prefectures where mixing tanks have not been upgraded since the 1970s.
* '''Hydrogen sulfide poisoning:''' Sulfur-type springs release hydrogen sulfide gas, which can accumulate in enclosed indoor bathing areas. In 2021, two bathers at a facility in [[Nagato Prefecture]] lost consciousness due to gas buildup; both survived but the incident prompted mandatory ventilation standards for all indoor sulfur onsen.
* '''Earthquake vulnerability:''' The [[2015 Suzukake Earthquake]] damaged 89 onsen facilities across three prefectures, destroying several historic bathhouses and temporarily altering the flow of 14 spring sources. The event exposed the vulnerability of unreinforced masonry bath structures and led to revised seismic standards for onsen construction.
* '''Overtourism:''' Several major onsen towns, particularly [[Shirakawa-Yunoyama]] in [[Nagayama Prefecture]], have experienced severe overcrowding during peak seasons. The influx of tourists has strained local infrastructure, increased waste output, and disrupted the {{nihongo|''tōji''|湯治|therapeutic cure stay}} tradition that requires quiet, extended stays for medical benefit.

